Sexual and reproductive health and rights entails a set of freedoms and entitlements. The freedoms include the right to make free and responsible decisions and choices, free of violence, coercion and discrimination, regarding matters concerning one’s body and sexual and reproductive health. The entitlements include unhindered access to a whole range of health facilities, goods, services and information, which ensure all people full enjoyment of sexual and reproductive health and rights. Access to sexual and reproductive healthcare lies at the very heart of women’s and girls’ universal and fundamental rights to equality, privacy and bodily integrity, and are critical to the enjoyment of all other rights, such as their right to health, to education, work and an adequate standard of living, [...] Lack of access to and poor quality services;
 Subjecting women’s access to services to third party authorization, such as parental, spousal and judicial authorization requirements for access to sexual and reproductive health services and information, including for abortion care and contraception;
 Discriminatory laws and practices in the area of sexual and reproductive health, such as criminalization of safe abortion care;
 Coercive or forced medical interventions, including forced sterilization or mandatory HIV/AIDS, virginity or pregnancy testing;
 Female genital mutilation (FGM); and
 Forced and unintended pregnancies.

### OHCHR’s work on sexual and reproductive health and rights [...] ### About sexual and reproductive health and rights

The rights of women and girls to the highest attainable standards of health, including sexual and reproductive health, are firmly grounded in international and regional human rights instruments.  These rights have been reaffirmed in consensus agreements established during the International Conference on Population and Development, the Beijing Platform for Action and the 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development, and recognized by international, regional and national human rights mechanisms and jurisprudence. This means that States have obligations to respect, protect and fulfill rights related to women’s and girls’ sexual and reproductive health.

